<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cercamon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1135–1145</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cercamon was one of the earliest troubadours, active in Aquitaine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> around 1135–1145. A handful of his songs survive, including a lament on the death of Duke William X of Aquitaine in 1137. His medieval biography calls him the teacher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcabru">Marcabru</a>, and his name, meaning 'search-world', suggests a wandering jongleur.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cercamon.jpg" alt="Cercamon" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BnF_ms._854_fol._133_-_Cercamon_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
